------------------------------------- Internationalization ------------------------------------------

- LOCALIZE THE APPLICATION WITH TRANSLOCO, LOCALE-AWARE FORMATTING, AND A PERSISTED LANGUAGE SWITCHER.

- Instructions: Angular Prerequisite "Dependency injection and services" chapter, "Forms" chapter, 
    "I18n" chapter.

- We’ve coded all our application in English, but let’s face it: a large part of our audience lives
    in France, and French people are not well-known for their English skills.
    🤩Let’s internationalize our application.

- It’s a small app, managed by a small team, supporting only English and French. ⚠️So instead of using
    Angular’s native i18n support, we’ll choose a lighter way, where everything is done at runtime,
    using a popular external library: Transloco.


1️⃣ 🔥🔥Translating texts using Transloco simply consists in configuring the application to load a
    JSON file containing translations for the configured language, and then using Angular directives,
    pipes and services, and pass them an i18n key to get the corresponding message from the JSON
    file.
    Let’s start by adding Transloco and its persist-lang plugin to the dependencies of the
    application:

        npm install --save-exact @jsverse/transloco@8.3.0 @jsverse/transloco-persist-lang@8.3.0
    
    or just:

        npm install @jsverse/transloco
        npm install @jsverse/transloco-persist-lang



2️⃣ 🔥We can now configure the application to use it. 🔥We’ll need to define a loader to load the JSON
    file for a given language, 🔥and add the Transloco provider to the application. 💡A traditional
    approach is to simply load the JSON file using the HttpClient and to store the files in the
    assets of the application. An issue with this approach is that the content of these files tends
    to change at each version of the application, so those files may not be cached reliably by the
    browser. Instead of using an HTTP loader, we’ll load the JSON files using a dynamic JavaScript
    import (the same ones we use to lazy-load components and routes). The JSON files will then be
    transformed into JavaScript bundles by the CLI.
    Add the following loader to the app.config.ts file:

        @Service()
        export class TranslocoModuleLoader implements TranslocoLoader {
            getTranslation(lang: string): Promise<Translation> {
                return import(`./i18n/${lang}.json`).then((m: { default: Translation }) => m.default);
            }
        }
    
    Then add the following provider to the app configuration:

        provideTransloco({
            config: {
                availableLangs: ['en', 'ne'],
                defaultLang: 'en',
                prodMode: !isDevMode()
            },
            loader: TranslocoModuleLoader
        })
    
    ❓What have we done?
    ✅We have configured Transloco to support two languages (en and ne), and to use our module
    loader which will locate the JSON files in src/app/i18n/en.json and src/app/i18n/ne.json
    respectively.



3️⃣ Let’s translate our home page. We have only a few texts to translate on this page: the
    subtitle, and the text of the various links. Let’s extract the English texts to the
    i18n/en.json file:

        {
            "home": {
                "subtitle": "Always a pleasure to bet on ponies",
                "login": "Login",
                "register": "Register",
                "races": "Races"
            }
        }

    And let’s play the role of the translator by also providing the French translations in i18n/fr.json file:
    i18n/fr.json:

        {
            "home": {
                "subtitle": "घोडा दौडमा बाजी लगाउन सधैं रमाइलो लाग्छ।",
                "login": "लगइन",
                "register": "दर्ता गर्नुहोस्",
                "races": "दौडहरू"
            }
        }



4️⃣ It’s now time to replace the hard-coded texts in the home component template with
    internationalized keys. To do that, Transloco provides a structural directive, which allows
    exposing a function (traditionally named t()) to the template. Here’s how it’s used:

        <ng-container *transloco="let t">
            The whole template goes here.
            It can use {{ t('the.i18n.key') }} to get an i18n message.
        </ng-container>
    
    home.html:

        <ng-container *transloco="let t">
            <div class="text-center" style="margin-top: 100px">
                <h1>Ponyracer <small>{{ t('home.subtitle') }}</small></h1>
            </div>

            <div class="text-center">
                @if (!user()) {
                    <a class="btn btn-large btn-primary me-2" routerLink="/login">{{ t('home.login') }}</a>
                    <a class="btn btn-large btn-primary" routerLink="/register">{{ t('home.register') }}</a>
                } @else {
                    <div class="text-center">
                        <a class="btn btn-large btn-primary" routerLink="/races">{{ t('home.races') }}</a>
                    </div>
                }
            </div>
        </ng-container>

    🔥Use it to translate the home page (⚠️don’t forget to import the TranslocoDirective), and check
    that everything is fine.



5️⃣ This is good, ❓but how can we see the page in French rather than the default English? There are
    various possible strategies. We’ll choose a 🔥simple one: configure the default language based
    on the browser preferred language. Transloco provides a helper function to get the preferred
    language of the browser: getBrowserLang(). If it is 'fr', we’ll use 'fr'. Otherwise we’ll use
    'en'. Change the code in app.config.ts to achieve that. Once it’s done, you can configure your
    browser to use French as a preferred language and reload the home page. It should now display
    its messages in French.

    You should now have a basic understanding of how it works to translate messages using Transloco.
    Of course, there is more: parameterized messages, translating from the TypeScript code, etc.
    Check the Transloco documentation(https://jsverse.github.io/transloco/home) to know all about it.

    🔥Internationalization isn’t limited to just translating text. It also has to do with formatting
    numbers and dates for example. In fact, we already do that with our fromNow pipe, but it’s
    hard-coded to use English for now. Transloco provides number and date formatting pipes, but
    what if we want to keep using the native Angular pipes? Those of course do not use the default
    language configured for Transloco. They use the LOCALE_ID injection token. And date-fns, that
    we use in the fromNow pipe, uses yet another source to get its locale. So we need to keep
    everything consistent.

    Let’s start by configuring the LOCALE_ID. In app.config.ts, add a provider for the LOCALE_ID
    token, using useValue for now. And use the default language that we’ve told Transloco to use
    before.

    🔥Also, don’t forget to load the locale data that Angular needs to format numbers and dates in
    French. To do that, add the following import at the beginning of the app.config.ts file:

        import '@angular/common/locales/global/fr';
    
    Let’s now use the appropriate locale in the fromNow pipe. You’ll need to pass an additional
    option named locale to the formatDistanceToNowStrict function. The value of this option is a
    Locale object that needs to be imported from 'date-fns/locale'. Inject the LOCALE_ID in the
    pipe, and depending on its value, pass the locale enUS or fr as the locale option of
    formatDistanceToNowStrict (locale is an option like addSuffix).
    from-now-pipe.ts:

        import { enUS, ne } from 'date-fns/locale';

        const locales: Record<string, Locale> = {
            en: enUS,
            ne: ne
        };

        @Pipe({
            name: 'prettyDate'
        })
        export class FromNowPipe implements PipeTransform {
            localeId = inject(LOCALE_ID);
            transform(value: string): string {
                const date = parseISO(value);
                return formatDistanceToNowStrict(date, { addSuffix: true, locale: locales[this.localeId] });
            }
        }

    ⚠️⚠️date-fns does not include nepali language support. It's just for the clarity. you can
    add other language support in the same way

    🔥🔥We could also have configured date-fns to use the configured locale by default, and leave
    the fromNow pipe as it was. This would have been a better solution, but we wanted to show you
    how to get the value of LOCALE_ID in case you need it.




6️⃣ Final step: we would like to let the users choose the language instead of relying on the browser
    default language. ⚠️The main issue with this feature is that Angular doesn’t allow changing the
    locale at runtime. The LOCALE_ID must be provided when the application is bootstrapped.

    In the menu component, just before the <ul> displaying the user information, add a
    <div class="navbar-nav"> , containing a select box displaying the available languages.
    Of course, the current language must be selected:

        <div class="navbar-nav">
            <select class="form-select" [formField]="languageForm.lang" aria-label="Language">
                @for (lang of availableLangs; track lang) {
                    <option [value]="lang">{{ lang }}</option>
                }
            </select>
        </div>
    
    menu.ts:

        @Component({
            imports: [RouterLink, DecimalPipe, NgbCollapse, FormField],
            // omitted
        })

        export class Menu {
            private readonly i18nService = inject(I18nService);
            protected readonly availableLangs = this.i18nService.availableLangs;
            protected readonly languageForm = form(
                signal({
                    lang: this.i18nService.lang
                })
            );

            constructor() {
                effect(() => {
                    this.i18nService.changeLanguage(this.languageForm.lang().value());
                });
            }
            // omitted
        }
    

    🔥🔥When the user chooses another language using that select box, we will ask Transloco to
    change the active language and reload the page. The persist-lang plugin will store the selected
    language in local storage under its default key and use it again when the application starts.
    In order to keep the code testable and to avoid duplicating the list of available languages,
    we will create a service. Create it with: ng generate service i18n-service.

    The file should have two exported constants in addition to the service class:
    👉 an exported injection token WINDOW that returns the browser window, so that we can easily
        test the service;
    👉 an exported readonly constant AVAILABLE_LANGS containing the array of available languages
        ('en' and 'fr');

    i18n-service.ts:

        export const AVAILABLE_LANGS: ReadonlyArray<string> = ['en', 'fr'];
            export const WINDOW = new InjectionToken<Window>('Window', {
            factory: () => window
        });

    This service should have:
    👉 a private translocoService field initialized with inject(TranslocoService);
    👉 a private window field initialized with inject(WINDOW);
    👉 a readonly property lang containing the language of the application. This property should
        be initialized when the service is constructed, using the active language from TranslocoService.
    👉 a method changeLanguage(lang: string), which does nothing if the given language is already
        the current language, and otherwise sets the active language in TranslocoService and then
        reloads the page, using this.window.location.reload().

    In addition, when the service is constructed, it should also set the lang property of
    this.window.document.documentElement, so that the browser knows that the page is using that
    language.

    Once the service is created, we can use it in the menu component.
    i18n-service.ts:

        export class I18nService {
            private readonly translocoService = inject(TranslocoService);
            private readonly window = inject(WINDOW);

            readonly availableLangs = AVAILABLE_LANGS;
            readonly lang = this.translocoService.getActiveLang();

            constructor() {
                this.window.document.documentElement.lang = this.lang;
            }

            changeLanguage(lang: string) {
                if (lang === this.lang) {
                    return;
                }
                this.translocoService.setActiveLang(lang);
                this.window.location.reload();
            }
        }



7️⃣ We can now replace the earlier browser-language default logic with provideTranslocoPersistLang().
    Use the AVAILABLE_LANGS constant to configure Transloco, add provideTranslocoPersistLang() to
    choose the cached language or browser language when the application starts, and provide the
    LOCALE_ID with a factory that reads the active language from TranslocoService:
    app.config.ts:

        provideTransloco({
            config: {
                availableLangs: [...AVAILABLE_LANGS],
                defaultLang: 'en',
                prodMode: !isDevMode()
            },
            loader: TranslocoModuleLoader
        }),
        provideTranslocoPersistLang({
            getLangFn: ({ cachedLang, browserLang, defaultLang }) => {
                const lang = cachedLang ?? browserLang ?? defaultLang;
                return AVAILABLE_LANGS.includes(lang) ? lang : defaultLang;
            },
            storage: {
                useValue: localStorage
            }
        }),
        { 
            provide: LOCALE_ID,
            useFactory: () => inject(TranslocoService).getActiveLang()
        }
    

    That’s it! Of course in the real world we would need to translate everything, but we’ve covered
    the most important aspects in this exercise:
    👉 integrate and configure Transloco to translate messages;
    👉 configure the LOCALE_ID to tell Angular which locale to use;
    👉 inject the LOCALE_ID where needed to configure other external libraries needing a locale.



8️⃣ Transloco has great support for testing as well. You can have a look at the tests provided with
    this exercise to see how we can test things in several languages, especially the
    i18n-test-utils.ts file. We also translated all the components in the solution, so you can
    learn a few more tricks, for example how to configure chartjs.


